changes={
2:'A 7816-byte initialized writable-data section in both existing source and split objects. It contains the 616-byte stroke atlas at offset 0, four eight-byte GlyphEntry records at 616, and the 7168-byte HSD_DebugFontAtlas at 648. Stroke records 0-46 have 13-byte stride and verified even-offset 0xFF terminators; five bytes remain after those 47 records. Each bitmap glyph is 56 bytes, yielding 128 glyphs. No explicit 32-byte atlas alignment is declared.',
3:'Owns two font representations and the default pixel table: a nibble-coordinate stroke atlas for DrawASCII, four packed-color/callback entries for software pixel output, and 128 packed bitmap glyphs supplied by debug_font.inc. Existing object sizes and offsets distinguish these three objects.',
4:'Setup writes two retained f32 glyph scales and converts the integer line_width to the retained u8. It passes that byte to line sizing and twice that byte, converted again to u8, to point sizing. DrawASCII reads the scales for point and line coordinates and returns horizontal scale as advance.',
6:'Two initialized f32 values 12.0 at offsets 0 and 4, followed by a u8 value 12 at offset 8. The source object holds nine bytes; split allocation holds sixteen including seven trailing padding bytes. No performance mode, visibility, background or palette objects are owned by this .sdata section.',
8:'Defaults are 12.0 for both scales and byte width 12. Setup unconditionally overwrites them, truncating integer line_width to u8; point size receives twice that byte through another u8 parameter. No range validation or prior-state restoration is provided.',
9:'The drawing helpers read numeric literals for glyph fractions, parametric positions, length calculation, tick sizes and conversion arithmetic. Current objects contain an 80-byte pool with matching bytes, not the old split-owned duplicate arrays or a GXColor object. Source ELF flags include WRITE; split flags omit WRITE. No owned drawing code mutates the pool.',
11:'An 80-byte heterogeneous numeric pool: f64 0.9,0.3,0.7 at offsets 0,8,16; f32 0.11 at 24; conversion bits 0x4330000080000000 at 32; f64 zero at 40; f32 zero at 48; f64 0.5 and 3 at 56 and 64; f32 6 and 4 at 72 and 76, with alignment padding at 28 and 52. No GXColor or five explicit one-element arrays are present in this current TU allocation.',
12:'Supplies numeric constants for glyph placement, parametric segment positions, Newton-Raphson length calculation and tick dimensions. Current pool ownership excludes the diagnostic compositor color and historical duplicate scalar-array claims.',
21:'Emits one four-vertex GX quad with a uniform supplied RGBA color, computing opposite coordinates as x+w and y+h. Vertex order is (x,y),(x+w,y),(x+w,y+h),(x,y+h); screen-space winding depends on extent signs and the active coordinate convention.',
23:'Stores caller scales and byte-truncated stroke width, loads current-camera viewing matrix zero, and installs direct position/color state with line/point sizes. DrawASCII uses those scales and the GX primitives use the configured state. The software XFB glyph rasterizers bypass GX and do not consume this setup.',
26:'Signature void(float scale_x,float scale_y,int line_width). The floats set glyph scales; line_width converts to u8 before line sizing. Point sizing receives twice the stored byte via a u8 parameter, so values can wrap again. This does not validate a positive geometric width.',
29:'Ordinary signed-byte characters are passed to DrawASCII and advance x by its returned scale. Backslash-c/C reads six following bytes and writes caller RGB, forcing alpha=255. The actual hexval maps A-F/a-f to 10-15 but maps ASCII 0-9 to zero; byte values 0-9 instead take ch-0x30. These controls therefore do not implement ordinary six-digit hexadecimal parsing.',
33:'NULL str returns (s32)x. Ordinary bytes draw with a copied current color. Backslash consumes its next selector; c/C additionally reads six bytes and mutates caller color, while unknown selectors draw nothing. There is no escape-length check: a trailing backslash or short color sequence can read beyond the NUL terminator. The final float x converts to s32 without range checks.',
34:'Reads eight-byte records containing an f32 parameter and packed RGBA word, including the color word before testing a negative parameter sentinel. Every parameter not less than zero, including NaN, produces a segment from the previous point to origin plus parameter times endpoint delta. Both endpoints use the same record color; no color interpolation occurs.',
36:'Canonical signature void(const u8* list,f32 x1,f32 y1,f32 x2,f32 y2). The implementation interprets each eight-byte record as f32 position plus four RGBA bytes. It stops on position<0 and returns for NULL list. This is a structural reading aid, not an attested GradientStop type; aligned valid records including the sentinel color word are required by the casts and load order.',
38:'NULL list returns. Each loop reads both fields of an eight-byte record before testing position<0. Otherwise it draws one uniform-color segment and updates its local previous point. There is no count bound, monotonicity check or clamp to [0,1], and NaN does not terminate. GX state must already be prepared.',
39:'Computes dx=x2-x1 and dy=y2-y1, approximates length with three reciprocal-square-root refinements and single-precision rounding, then forms (-dy/len,-dx/len). This vector is not generally perpendicular to (dx,dy), though it is for axis-aligned baselines. Four- and six-unit multiples form ticks; baseline steps are dx/count and dy/count.',
42:'Draws a colored baseline and ticks at integer indices i whose float conversion is <= count, stepping by delta/count. Tick direction is (-dy/len,-dx/len), not a general perpendicular. Index zero and every fifth index use the longer six-unit multiple; other indices use four units. A noninteger count does not generally place a tick exactly at the endpoint.',
43:'Emits the baseline before the inclusive integer-index tick loop. Length normalization happens without a zero-length guard; each tick ends with division by count, including count zero. Negative count skips ticks after baseline; extreme or nonfinite inputs are not validated. No graphics-state setup or restoration is performed.',
49:'For ordinary supported coordinates, the console passes a packed glyph, origin, framebuffer bounds/stride and optional four-entry callback table. The rasterizer skips negative-origin rows/columns and decodes two-bit samples into callback entries. However its unsigned max_x calculation precedes x clamping: x<-11 with positive width makes max_x=width, so a fully left-offscreen glyph can trigger reads beyond its nominal cell.',
52:'Canonical signature void(void* bitmap,s32 x,s32 y,s32 dst,s32 w,s32 h,s32 stride,void* tbl). It reads a 56-byte, four-byte-per-row packed glyph, normally rendering 11 columns across 14 rows. The header comment says 12 used pixels, but the implementation and console advance use 11. NULL table selects four default entries. Target 32-bit addresses and aligned u32 source reads are assumed.',
53:'Decodes nominally 11-by-14 two-bit glyph samples for progressive framebuffer output and dispatches each sample to a table-selected callback. It implements partial clipping but is not a general safe clipped blitter: sufficiently negative x and invalid signed dimensions can defeat the unsigned comparisons.',
54:'Substitutes default table for NULL, clips negative y by source-row skipping, and computes max_x using unsigned x+11 before clamping negative x to zero. For ordinary dimensions and x>=-11, this limits columns; for x<-11, max_x can become full width and source reads can extend beyond the glyph. Rows advance source four bytes and destination y by one while off_y<14 and unsigned y<h. No bitmap, destination, callback, dimension or stride validation exists.',
55:'Uses caller parity and shared four-entry pixel-table format to select alternating source rows and field-row addresses. Negative y skips source rows; a parity mismatch adds one row; negative x is clamped after the unsigned right-edge calculation. The same fully-left x<-11 failure as the progressive path can permit reads beyond the nominal glyph.',
58:'Canonical signature void(void* bitmap,s32 x,s32 y,s32 parity,s32 dst,s32 w,s32 h,s32 stride,void* tbl). Packed source rows are four bytes, nominal display is 11-by-14, and destination field rows use (y/2)*stride. Parity is expected to be 0 or 1 but is not validated or normalized. NULL table selects defaults; the declared generic pointers and signed 32-bit address remain unchanged.',
59:'Decodes nominally 11-by-14 packed glyphs into alternating rows of an interlaced field, dispatching samples to selected callbacks. Clipping and parity handling assume valid caller inputs; the unsigned right-edge calculation is unsafe for sufficiently negative x, and arbitrary parity values are not rejected.',
60:'After optional negative-y skipping, increments y and source offset once if y%2 differs from parity. It never checks parity is 0/1. Computes unsigned right bound before x clamping, so x<-11 can decode beyond the cell. Each emitted row advances source offset and screen y by two and stored row index by one. NULL table selects defaults; other pointers, signed bounds, stride and callbacks are unvalidated.',
61:'GX callers supply coordinates, characters, scales and colors that become immediate vertices. Software framebuffer paths read packed samples and call one of four pixel entries using computed two-byte destinations. Nominal glyph dimensions are 11-by-14; partial clipping includes an unsigned-bound failure for fully left-offscreen origins, so it does not guarantee bounded input or output memory access.',
65:'Retains two f32 scales and u8 line width initialized to 12; GX setup replaces them and changes graphics state. Software glyph calls select default/custom tables and perform partial clipping, with an unsigned right-bound failure when x<-11. The interlaced path conditionally advances one initial row and then every second row; it assumes a valid 0/1 parity. Shared GX setup is separate from these direct-memory writes.'}
